<%# What the Markdown component needs from the host, what it lets
    through, and how a reply still being written is rendered. %>
<%
  # Heredocs live inside ONE scriptlet tag. A literal ERB closer would end
  # this scriptlet early, so ERB samples interpolate it as #{e}; a literal opener would nest an ERB tag under Herb, so they interpolate it as #{o}.
  e = "%" + ">"
  o = "<" + "%"
  gem_source = <<~CODE
    # Gemfile
    gem "commonmarker"

    # config/initializers/poetry.rb - only when you want something other than the default
    Poetry::Ui::MarkdownParser.parser = :redcarpet                      # both gems bundled, this one preferred
    Poetry::Ui::MarkdownParser.renderer = ->(text) { MyParser.to_html(text) } # a parser of your own
  CODE
  stream_source = <<~CODE
    # app/jobs/chat_response_job.rb - re-render the reply's row as its text grows
    text = +""
    chat.ask(prompt) do |chunk|
      text << chunk.content.to_s
      message.broadcast_replace_to(chat, partial: "messages/reply", locals: { message: message, text: text, streaming: true })
    end
    message.broadcast_replace_to(chat, partial: "messages/reply", locals: { message: message, text: text, streaming: false })
  CODE
  reply_source = <<~CODE
    #{o}# app/views/messages/_reply.html.erb #{e}
    #{o}= poetry_message_scroller_item(id: message.id, dom_id: dom_id(message), class: "px-4 py-2") do #{e}
      #{o}= poetry_message do #{e}
        #{o}= poetry_bubble(variant: :ghost) do #{e}
          #{o}= poetry_markdown(text: text, streaming: streaming, heading_level: 3) #{e}
        #{o} end #{e}
      #{o} end #{e}
    #{o} end #{e}
  CODE
%>
<section class="flex flex-col gap-3">
  <h2 class="text-lg font-medium" id="a-markdown-gem">A Markdown gem</h2>
  <p class="max-w-2xl text-muted-foreground">
    Parsing is the host's gem, the way highlighting is for CodeBlock. With
    <code class="text-foreground">commonmarker</code> or
    <code class="text-foreground">redcarpet</code> in the Gemfile the text is parsed as
    GitHub-flavored Markdown: tables, strikethrough and bare addresses as links.
    <code class="text-foreground">commonmarker</code> is used when both are there. Without either,
    the text renders as plain paragraphs with its line breaks kept and its fenced code still a code
    block, so a page never fails for want of a gem.
  </p>
  <%= poetry_code_block(code: gem_source, language: "ruby", label: "The gem, and naming another") %>
</section>

<section class="flex flex-col gap-3">
  <h2 class="text-lg font-medium" id="what-reaches-the-page">What reaches the page</h2>
  <p class="max-w-2xl text-muted-foreground">
    The parser's HTML is never passed on. The component reads it and writes each element again from
    a short list: headings, paragraphs, emphasis, inline code, quotes, lists, rules, tables, links
    and images. An element it does not know is reduced to its text, and no attribute survives except
    a link's address and title, an image's address and alt text, a list's starting number and a
    cell's alignment. HTML written in the text is shown as text, so a reply can still name a tag.
    This holds whatever wrote the HTML, a parser of your own included.
  </p>
  <ul class="max-w-2xl list-disc space-y-1 ps-5 text-muted-foreground">
    <li>
      A link keeps an <code class="text-foreground">http</code>,
      <code class="text-foreground">https</code>, <code class="text-foreground">mailto</code> or
      <code class="text-foreground">tel</code> address, or one on your own site. Any other scheme
      leaves the label as plain text. Every link carries
      <code class="text-foreground">rel="nofollow noopener noreferrer"</code>, and
      <code class="text-foreground">link_target: "_blank"</code> opens them in a new tab.
    </li>
    <li>
      An image renders as a link to it. A reader's browser fetches a remote image without asking,
      which a reply shaped by untrusted content can use to send data out, so
      <code class="text-foreground">images: true</code> is a choice, and then only an absolute
      <code class="text-foreground">http</code> or <code class="text-foreground">https</code>
      address loads.
    </li>
    <li>Fenced code is a CodeBlock in the fence's language, with its copy button.</li>
    <li>A table sits in a scroller, so a wide one scrolls sideways and the prose keeps its width.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section class="flex flex-col gap-3">
  <h2 class="text-lg font-medium" id="a-reply-being-written">A reply still being written</h2>
  <p class="max-w-2xl text-muted-foreground">
    A reply arrives as a growing piece of its Markdown, and a piece ends wherever the last token
    did: inside a bold phrase, halfway through a link's address, one row into a table. Rendered as
    it stands, each of those shows its raw marks until the closing ones arrive, then jumps.
    <code class="text-foreground">streaming: true</code> closes what the last block has left open
    before it is parsed, so every frame reads as finished prose.
  </p>
  <ul class="max-w-2xl list-disc space-y-1 ps-5 text-muted-foreground">
    <li>Bold, italic and strikethrough are closed where the text stops.</li>
    <li>A code span is closed; code inside an open fence is left alone, since it already renders as code.</li>
    <li>A link whose address is not finished shows its label, and an image shows nothing yet.</li>
    <li>A table is a table from its first row, before the row under it arrives.</li>
    <li>A mark with nothing after it yet is left out until something follows.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="max-w-2xl text-muted-foreground">
    The server holds the text. Each time it grows, render the reply's row again and replace it, as
    the MessageScroller asks for any streamed row, then render the finished reply once more without
    <code class="text-foreground">streaming:</code>. The closing is for display only: a finished
    reply renders the same either way.
  </p>
  <p class="max-w-2xl text-muted-foreground">
    A reply that was stopped, or that failed part way, is never finished, and a host usually keeps
    what was written. Store what <code class="text-foreground">Poetry::Ui::MarkdownTail.close(text)</code>
    returns, so the saved text is whole Markdown and renders the same wherever it is shown, or keep
    rendering that message with <code class="text-foreground">streaming: true</code>.
  </p>
  <%= poetry_code_block(code: stream_source, language: "ruby", label: "The job") %>
  <%= poetry_code_block(code: reply_source, language: "erb", label: "The row") %>
</section>

<section class="flex flex-col gap-3">
  <h2 class="text-lg font-medium" id="headings-in-a-transcript">Headings in a transcript</h2>
  <p class="max-w-2xl text-muted-foreground">
    A model writes <code class="text-foreground">#</code> for its top heading, which would put a
    second <code class="text-foreground">h1</code> on your page.
    <code class="text-foreground">heading_level: 3</code> renders that heading as an
    <code class="text-foreground">h3</code> and the ones under it one level down each, none past
    <code class="text-foreground">h6</code>. The component is a Typeset, so
    <code class="text-foreground">preset:</code> retunes its rhythm as it does there, and it takes
    the size of the text around it.
  </p>
</section>
